WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.839
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:04.109 --> 00:04.909
‎TOKYO-HAKONE ‎ROUND-TRIP COLLEGE EKIDEN RACE

3
00:04.976 --> 00:07.912
The Hakone Ekiden was founded in 1920.

4
00:07.979 --> 00:09.781
Nice shot on Camera One!

5
00:09.848 --> 00:12.283
-At Dainichi TV, the crew… -Show lineup. Graphics.

6
00:12.784 --> 00:16.521
-…led by chief producer Ryo Tokushige -Ten seconds to air!

7
00:16.588 --> 00:19.057
was pressured by ratings-focused executives

8
00:19.124 --> 00:21.626
to make the broadcast more entertaining.

9
00:21.693 --> 00:22.560
Idiot!

10
00:22.627 --> 00:25.730
The Hakone Ekiden doesn't belong to you!

11
00:25.797 --> 00:28.833
It's Dainichi TV's precious asset!

12
00:28.900 --> 00:30.201
It belongs to

13
00:30.268 --> 00:31.736
the runners.

14
00:31.803 --> 00:33.338
Tokushige's decision

15
00:33.404 --> 00:36.641
shook the entire network.

16
00:36.708 --> 00:39.644
At the sponsor's request since the first broadcast,

17
00:39.711 --> 00:42.614
they were promised that the broadcast

18
00:42.680 --> 00:43.615
could continue as usual.

19
00:43.681 --> 00:44.782
Yeah!

20
00:44.849 --> 00:47.418
Meanwhile, Meisei Gakuin failed to qualify

21
00:47.485 --> 00:51.022
after captain Hayato Aoba faded.

22
00:51.089 --> 00:52.724
Legendary coach Hisashige Moroya named

23
00:52.790 --> 00:55.093
inexperienced Masato Kai

24
00:55.160 --> 00:57.028
as his successor.

25
00:57.095 --> 00:58.463
We're going back to Hakone.

26
00:58.530 --> 01:00.732
That's my only goal.

27
01:00.798 --> 01:03.234
In an unusual move, Kai was also put in charge of

28
01:03.301 --> 01:05.970
the Kanto Student United Team

29
01:06.037 --> 01:06.905
Can you do it?

30
01:06.971 --> 01:08.139
I can.

31
01:08.706 --> 01:11.276
The battle of runners who had already tasted defeat

32
01:11.342 --> 01:13.545
was about to begin.

33
01:21.252 --> 01:24.522
STUDENT UNITED TEAM MEETING DAY

34
01:24.589 --> 01:25.523
THE STUDENT UNITED TEAM

35
01:25.590 --> 01:27.358
THE 21ST TEAM OF RUNNERS WHO FAILED TO QUALIFY

36
01:27.425 --> 01:28.593
CAMP AT TOHO ECONOMICS

37
01:28.660 --> 01:29.494
It's finally time.

38
01:30.061 --> 01:31.496
MEISEI GAKUIN CAPTAIN HAYATO AOBA

39
01:31.563 --> 01:33.097
The 10,000-kilometer…

40
01:33.665 --> 01:35.266
You mean 10,000 meters.

41
01:35.333 --> 01:37.936
That's Keisuke Nogi, a first-year at Keisei University…

42
01:38.002 --> 01:39.270
-You said 10,000 kilometers. -No.

43
01:39.337 --> 01:40.705
…and Daichi Murai, a third-year

44
01:40.772 --> 01:42.473
at Toho University of Economics.

45
01:43.041 --> 01:45.743
They're the top runners with the two best times.

46
01:48.947 --> 01:50.415
SEIWA INTERNATIONAL KOTA MATSUKI

47
01:50.481 --> 01:52.016
KANTO UNIVERSITY OF CULTURE SEIYA NAITO/HARUKA MOMOYAMA/SHUTO TOMIOKA

48
01:52.083 --> 01:53.451
I hear some runners

49
01:54.018 --> 01:56.854
were reluctant to join the Student United Team.

50
01:57.655 --> 01:58.690
-Really? -Yes.

51
01:58.756 --> 01:59.591
Morning.

52
01:59.657 --> 02:01.926
-Good morning! -Good morning!

53
02:04.896 --> 02:08.299
I'm Kai, head coach of the Student United Team.

54
02:10.034 --> 02:11.169
Nice to meet you.

55
02:11.236 --> 02:14.639
I'm Onuma, one of your coaches, from Toho University of Economics.

56
02:14.706 --> 02:16.708
Let's run an exciting race together.

57
02:16.774 --> 02:19.477
Feel free to make full use of our facilities.

58
02:20.044 --> 02:23.281
I'm Kitano, also a coach, from Seiwa International University.

59
02:23.348 --> 02:24.215
KOICHI KITANO

60
02:24.282 --> 02:27.051
Let's make this a good experience that helps each of your teams.

61
02:27.118 --> 02:28.853
-Nice to meet you. -Nice to meet you.

62
02:32.690 --> 02:35.093
Tenma Isayama, fourth-year, Shinagawa Institute of Technology.

63
02:35.159 --> 02:35.994
TENMA ISAYAMA

64
02:36.060 --> 02:38.763
If I'm running Hakone, I want to be on TV and stand out!

65
02:38.830 --> 02:40.231
That's all I'm here for!

66
02:42.433 --> 02:43.268
Nice to meet you.

67
02:43.334 --> 02:46.404
I'm Jo Inomata, a second-year at Musashino Agricultural University.

68
02:46.471 --> 02:49.340
I'm 190 cm tall, and I'm confident in my leg strength.

69
02:50.041 --> 02:51.009
Nice to meet you.

70
02:51.075 --> 02:52.410
Nice to meet you.

71
02:52.910 --> 02:55.546
I'm Kota Matsuki, a fourth-year at Seiwa International University.

72
03:00.018 --> 03:00.852
Well…

73
03:02.820 --> 03:05.590
I hope to gain experience I can take back to my own team.

74
03:05.657 --> 03:06.491
Nice to meet you.

75
03:09.160 --> 03:10.428
Nice to meet you.

76
03:11.529 --> 03:12.764
Next, Aoba.

77
03:13.765 --> 03:14.599
Yes.

78
03:18.169 --> 03:19.003
I don't want

79
03:20.672 --> 03:22.307
the Student United Team

80
03:23.608 --> 03:26.110
to be just about gaining experience.

81
03:27.945 --> 03:30.048
Every other team

82
03:30.114 --> 03:32.016
will be going all out to win.

83
03:34.352 --> 03:35.653
I want us to be one team

84
03:36.854 --> 03:38.823
and truly compete against our rivals.

85
03:39.791 --> 03:40.792
That's what I believe.

86
03:53.104 --> 03:53.971
Oh, sorry.

87
03:56.674 --> 03:57.742
I'm Dan Kurashina,

88
03:57.809 --> 03:58.643
‎SECOND-YEAR AT SANNO UNIVERSITY ‎DAN KURASHINA

89
03:58.710 --> 04:00.111
a second-year at Sanno University.

90
04:00.945 --> 04:02.814
I wanted to run Hakone no matter what,

91
04:03.614 --> 04:06.551
so I'm real happy I was selected!

92
04:07.485 --> 04:08.386
Let's make these

93
04:08.453 --> 04:10.088
two months count!

94
04:11.656 --> 04:12.590
Nice to meet you.

95
04:15.560 --> 04:16.961
That's all 16 runners.

96
04:17.762 --> 04:20.565
Ten of you will run Hakone.

97
04:26.437 --> 04:28.840
Yano, anything to say?

98
04:29.841 --> 04:30.708
Yes.

99
04:31.776 --> 04:33.411
Keito Yano from Meisei Gakuin University.

100
04:33.478 --> 04:35.380
I'll be the team manager.

101
04:37.882 --> 04:39.283
I have one suggestion.

102
04:39.851 --> 04:41.753
Why don't we call each other by our first names?

103
04:42.487 --> 04:44.455
I think it would help us feel closer.

104
04:45.223 --> 04:47.825
It might be a little awkward at first, but…

105
04:49.026 --> 04:50.061
what does everyone think?

106
04:50.128 --> 04:51.963
-I'm in! -Then

107
04:52.563 --> 04:55.366
call me Makko-chan!

108
04:55.967 --> 04:57.168
Please call me

109
04:57.735 --> 04:59.303
Takkun!

110
05:00.304 --> 05:01.305
Thank you.

111
05:02.006 --> 05:04.809
Next, I'd like to choose a captain.

112
05:05.476 --> 05:06.978
Any volunteers?

113
05:31.135 --> 05:32.103
I'll volunteer.

114
05:36.908 --> 05:38.042
Thank you.

115
05:41.746 --> 05:42.947
Lastly,

116
05:43.648 --> 05:46.083
what is this Student United Team running for?

117
05:47.485 --> 05:50.621
To make that clear, I want to set a goal.

118
05:52.790 --> 05:53.891
My goal is

119
05:55.092 --> 05:56.894
a top-three finish at the Hakone Ekiden.

120
06:01.432 --> 06:02.567
Top three?

121
06:03.134 --> 06:04.168
Seriously?

122
06:05.136 --> 06:06.270
As you know,

123
06:06.337 --> 06:09.941
the Student United Team is a non-scoring entry. Our times aren't official.

124
06:11.876 --> 06:15.012
Even if we won, it would be a phantom victory.

125
06:16.280 --> 06:19.951
Still, the reason I've set this goal

126
06:20.751 --> 06:23.154
is that you gain nothing from a race you don't run for real.

127
06:25.423 --> 06:27.492
If you just want a taste of Hakone,

128
06:27.992 --> 06:29.861
just wave a flag by the side of the road.

129
06:32.230 --> 06:34.665
See you at the track in 30 minutes.

130
06:36.434 --> 06:37.502
-Yes. -Yes.

131
06:44.175 --> 06:47.311
56 DAYS UNTIL THE HAKONE EKIDEN

132
06:47.378 --> 06:49.914
There are too many captions for Keya's part. Watch that.

133
06:50.414 --> 06:53.384
Also, we interviewed Murakami, who couldn't run because of injury.

134
06:53.451 --> 06:54.318
We need to use that.

135
06:54.385 --> 06:55.353
Understood.

136
06:55.419 --> 06:56.954
I'll give you notes on the rest.

137
06:57.522 --> 06:58.823
I'm heading to the CG meeting.

138
07:00.491 --> 07:03.427
ASSISTANT DIRECTOR CHIKA TOYAMA

139
07:03.494 --> 07:05.096
Mr. Nomura,

140
07:05.730 --> 07:09.867
this is my fourth round of fixes on the website video.

141
07:11.502 --> 07:13.204
Is it really that bad?

142
07:13.704 --> 07:17.074
"Hakone Highlights to Study Up On."

143
07:17.141 --> 07:18.776
-So this is what you're doing? -Yes.

144
07:18.843 --> 07:19.944
Yes.

145
07:20.011 --> 07:21.879
-Everyone's shaping up. -Yeah.

146
07:21.946 --> 07:24.448
Maybe add captions to make it easier to follow?

147
07:24.515 --> 07:27.552
No, Natsuki doesn't seem to like captions much.

148
07:30.888 --> 07:33.090
Then hype it up with narration?

149
07:33.157 --> 07:36.661
She told me not to use too much narration either.

150
07:39.997 --> 07:42.033
CHIEF PRODUCER RYO TOKUSHIGE

151
07:42.099 --> 07:43.935
…15, 16,

152
07:44.535 --> 07:45.903
17, 18,

153
07:46.404 --> 07:49.273
19, 20, 21.

154
07:49.840 --> 07:51.275
It's a good pace!

155
07:51.909 --> 07:53.244
Stay relaxed!

156
07:58.115 --> 08:00.918
Those two really are fast…

157
08:00.985 --> 08:02.486
KEISUKE NOGI / DAICHI MURAI

158
08:02.553 --> 08:03.654
Lap 25,

159
08:03.721 --> 08:05.389
26, 27.

160
08:06.057 --> 08:06.891
Kota, pick it up!

161
08:07.458 --> 08:09.327
SEIWA INTERNATIONAL UNIVERSITY KOTA MATSUKI

162
08:12.830 --> 08:14.632
Just keep that pace.

163
08:19.403 --> 08:21.005
Haruka! Stay! Don't speed up now!

164
08:21.572 --> 08:25.076
THIRD-YEAR AT TOYO UNIVERSITY OF COMMERCE HARUKA MOMOYAMA

165
08:25.142 --> 08:27.812
…35, 36,

166
08:27.878 --> 08:30.481
37, 38, 39,

167
08:31.115 --> 08:33.618
40, 41, 42,

168
08:34.418 --> 08:36.454
43, 44…

169
08:36.520 --> 08:37.788
Nice.

170
08:48.366 --> 08:52.737
…46, 47, 48…

171
09:00.478 --> 09:02.880
You only get one shot.

172
09:07.485 --> 09:10.221
Also, send me your draft questions for the runner interviews.

173
09:10.287 --> 09:12.490
I want to check them beforehand.

174
09:13.524 --> 09:14.692
She sure doesn't trust us.

175
09:14.759 --> 09:15.660
‎ASSISTANT DIRECTOR ‎AYUMU HASHIGUCHI

176
09:15.726 --> 09:16.994
-Mr. Kuwana. -Yes.

177
09:17.061 --> 09:20.498
I need that camera operator list for the broadcast points soon.

178
09:20.564 --> 09:21.766
I know!

179
09:22.333 --> 09:24.669
-We've got a lot going on too. -Right?

180
09:24.735 --> 09:26.237
That's it for today's meeting.

181
09:26.303 --> 09:28.572
-Okay. -Okay.

182
09:30.474 --> 09:32.009
Oh, Toyama. Here.

183
09:32.643 --> 09:34.578
-What's this? -Fixes for that video segment.

184
09:35.613 --> 09:37.081
What, all of this?

185
09:37.648 --> 09:40.051
"Cut two. Too close on the runner's face." "Cut three,

186
09:40.117 --> 09:42.720
trim one second off the top. Cut four. Use a wider shot."

187
09:44.822 --> 09:47.058
I'm basically redoing the whole thing!

188
09:47.124 --> 09:47.992
Hey, let me see that.

189
09:51.062 --> 09:54.365
If this were for broadcast, sure. But it's just a video for the website.

190
09:55.099 --> 09:56.667
Why not let her do it her way?

191
09:57.368 --> 09:59.704
If you shoot down everything, she'll lose her motivation.

192
09:59.770 --> 10:02.773
I'm just pointing out what isn't working.

193
10:02.840 --> 10:03.674
Hey.

194
10:04.442 --> 10:07.745
I'm saying don't do everything your way.

195
10:07.812 --> 10:09.680
Try trusting other people for once.

196
10:10.815 --> 10:12.183
There's no right answer here.

197
10:14.885 --> 10:18.556
If everyone does whatever they want, the team won't come together.

198
10:19.056 --> 10:21.692
That's what the broadcast director is there for, I think.

199
10:21.759 --> 10:22.827
So what,

200
10:23.360 --> 10:25.763
we have to follow whatever you say?

201
10:26.564 --> 10:28.232
Is the broadcast director some kind of god?

202
10:34.205 --> 10:35.806
RAMEN

203
10:35.873 --> 10:37.208
I see…

204
10:37.875 --> 10:40.911
Hakone's less than two months away. This is really bad.

205
10:41.479 --> 10:43.748
So, what do you think?

206
10:43.814 --> 10:44.849
DIRECTOR TOUGO MATSUZAWA

207
10:44.915 --> 10:46.383
-Me? -Yeah.

208
10:48.119 --> 10:49.320
Well,

209
10:49.954 --> 10:52.823
I've known Ms. Miyamoto a long time,

210
10:52.890 --> 10:55.025
so I'm kind of used to it…

211
10:55.092 --> 10:58.362
And she has her good points…

212
10:58.429 --> 11:02.833
But honestly, I get where Mr. Nomura's coming from.

213
11:02.900 --> 11:05.236
Ms. Miyamoto's super-talented,

214
11:05.302 --> 11:07.271
but that makes her hard on people.

215
11:07.838 --> 11:10.908
If she praised us a little more, we'd be more motivated.

216
11:10.975 --> 11:12.076
That's what I think.

217
11:12.143 --> 11:14.545
Would it kill her

218
11:14.612 --> 11:16.013
to be a bit nicer

219
11:16.080 --> 11:19.383
with greetings and email replies?

220
11:19.450 --> 11:20.284
And another thing…

221
11:20.918 --> 11:22.453
I can't believe we have camp

222
11:22.520 --> 11:24.021
every single weekend!

223
11:24.755 --> 11:28.292
Usually the Student United Team meets only two or three times before the race.

224
11:28.359 --> 11:29.660
So this is pretty rare.

225
11:30.861 --> 11:32.963
But I'm glad we get to train a lot.

226
11:33.464 --> 11:34.465
Good work.

227
11:35.032 --> 11:36.133
Are you a manga character?

228
11:36.200 --> 11:38.202
Running makes me hungry.

229
11:38.869 --> 11:40.304
Let's eat.

230
11:40.371 --> 11:41.438
LEG PREFERENCE SURVEY

231
11:41.505 --> 11:43.607
Oh, the leg preference survey!

232
11:43.674 --> 11:45.442
Which leg does everyone want?

233
11:47.678 --> 11:48.512
For me,

234
11:48.579 --> 11:51.482
maybe the fifth or sixth leg. I'm good on hills.

235
11:51.549 --> 11:52.349
‎FIFTH LEG-ODAWARA TO LAKE ASHI ‎SIXTH LEG-LAKE ASHI TO ODAWARA

236
11:52.416 --> 11:55.386
Keisuke and Daichi are the favorites for the prestigious second leg.

237
11:55.452 --> 11:57.288
Look at you, top runners!

238
11:57.354 --> 11:58.656
Well, I don't know.

239
11:58.722 --> 12:00.925
I'll do my best. It's my dream.

240
12:01.559 --> 12:04.261
-Tenma, which leg do you want? -The first leg and the third leg.

241
12:04.762 --> 12:07.198
The start of the first leg is always on TV.

242
12:07.765 --> 12:10.501
If I'm on TV longer, my parents will be happy.

243
12:11.168 --> 12:12.536
And I can brag to my friends.

244
12:12.603 --> 12:14.238
Talk about shallow motives.

245
12:14.805 --> 12:17.107
Then the tenth leg at the end is always on TV too.

246
12:17.174 --> 12:18.175
Roadside crowds are huge.

247
12:18.242 --> 12:22.112
In the return race, they only film you if you're up front.

248
12:22.179 --> 12:23.781
Plus, we're the Student United Team.

249
12:23.848 --> 12:25.249
So why the third leg?

250
12:25.816 --> 12:28.519
The view, of course!

251
12:29.220 --> 12:31.222
The Shonan Coast. Mount Fuji.

252
12:31.288 --> 12:33.490
-It's the best! -That's why?

253
12:33.557 --> 12:35.092
Of course!

254
12:36.393 --> 12:37.628
Hey! What are you doing?

255
12:38.195 --> 12:39.063
What about you guys?

256
12:39.630 --> 12:40.731
Any legs you want?

257
12:42.233 --> 12:45.903
Whatever. We just run the leg we're assigned.

258
12:46.937 --> 12:48.439
I agree.

259
12:48.505 --> 12:51.275
Yeah, same for me, pretty much.

260
12:52.476 --> 12:53.544
Hey.

261
12:53.611 --> 12:55.512
You said the third leg has a nice view,

262
12:56.080 --> 12:57.915
but it's a pretty tough leg.

263
12:58.682 --> 13:02.419
The first half is a gentle downhill, but along the coast, the wind hits you.

264
13:02.486 --> 13:05.956
Temperatures swing a lot. Your legs could give out if you're careless.

265
13:07.691 --> 13:10.127
Hope you have time to look at Mount Fuji.

266
13:10.194 --> 13:13.097
It'd be the worst if you got dizzy

267
13:14.031 --> 13:15.299
and couldn't pass the sash.

268
13:16.033 --> 13:17.001
Well, see you.

269
13:19.970 --> 13:21.272
What was that?

270
13:24.441 --> 13:25.409
TOHO ECONOMICS

271
13:25.476 --> 13:26.443
Still,

272
13:26.510 --> 13:29.613
I was surprised by the top-three goal.

273
13:30.447 --> 13:34.118
Come to think of it, Coach Kai saw right through me.

274
13:35.019 --> 13:37.354
Today I hesitated over when to make my move.

275
13:39.924 --> 13:41.959
You only get one shot.

276
13:43.227 --> 13:45.029
It was the same when we picked the captain.

277
13:45.095 --> 13:46.730
Any volunteers?

278
13:49.466 --> 13:51.001
Coach and I made eye contact.

279
13:51.802 --> 13:54.438
And before I knew it, I'd raised my hand.

280
13:57.441 --> 13:58.542
I didn't plan to volunteer.

281
14:01.779 --> 14:04.515
But I was really happy

282
14:05.049 --> 14:07.117
that you volunteered to be captain.

283
14:08.118 --> 14:09.086
Thanks.

284
14:12.156 --> 14:16.727
But everyone's kind of all over the place.

285
14:19.930 --> 14:21.632
Can I really do this?

286
14:27.071 --> 14:29.473
I don't think it'll be easy.

287
14:30.240 --> 14:34.678
But I think you're the only one who can do it.

288
14:35.245 --> 14:37.781
I'll support the team with everything I have.

289
14:38.849 --> 14:39.683
Okay.

290
14:40.384 --> 14:42.886
What the heck?

291
14:42.953 --> 14:46.256
No one's filling in their work schedules. Seriously…

292
14:46.824 --> 14:48.759
Well,

293
14:48.826 --> 14:50.995
I guess this is part of

294
14:51.061 --> 14:52.863
a chief producer's job too…

295
14:54.098 --> 14:56.433
Mr. Tokushige, you always used to say

296
14:56.500 --> 14:58.035
the field was where you belonged.

297
14:59.236 --> 15:02.006
Running around in the field was fun.

298
15:02.606 --> 15:06.377
But once you're in management, there's a lot to deal with.

299
15:06.944 --> 15:08.879
Even when I get home exhausted,

300
15:09.380 --> 15:11.248
all a divorced guy like me has waiting

301
15:11.315 --> 15:14.685
is a lonely can of beer.

302
15:20.324 --> 15:21.191
Mr. Tokushige.

303
15:24.161 --> 15:27.097
Have you ever wanted to quit your job?

304
15:27.998 --> 15:29.299
Of course.

305
15:30.300 --> 15:32.202
I think about it every day.

306
15:35.172 --> 15:36.173
But

307
15:37.274 --> 15:40.978
in the end, I love this job.

308
15:43.213 --> 15:46.617
What about you? Ever wanted to quit?

309
15:49.353 --> 15:53.357
Maybe I'm not cut out for this job.

310
15:53.424 --> 15:54.625
What?

311
15:55.826 --> 15:57.661
Ever since I was a kid,

312
15:57.728 --> 16:00.731
I've been bad at working with others

313
16:01.265 --> 16:02.900
or bringing people together.

314
16:04.468 --> 16:06.870
I wish I could do it like you.

315
16:08.272 --> 16:09.173
I'm just good at

316
16:09.807 --> 16:11.675
finding people I trust

317
16:11.742 --> 16:12.976
and tossing things

318
16:13.744 --> 16:14.678
their way.

319
16:16.713 --> 16:17.915
"Toss-It-All Tokushige."

320
16:19.883 --> 16:21.351
That's what they used to call you.

321
16:21.985 --> 16:23.087
Did they?

322
16:23.787 --> 16:25.422
When you were broadcast director

323
16:25.489 --> 16:27.591
and I was the director in Car One.

324
16:30.260 --> 16:32.696
Miyamoto! The lead's changing!

325
16:32.763 --> 16:34.164
Stay on Car One for a while!

326
16:34.998 --> 16:37.134
It's all yours!

327
16:39.536 --> 16:40.370
Yes.

328
16:40.938 --> 16:42.539
Car One had three cameras,

329
16:43.240 --> 16:44.808
and I panicked over

330
16:45.409 --> 16:46.877
which one to take.

331
16:49.413 --> 16:50.380
But…

332
16:51.582 --> 16:52.549
I was

333
16:53.317 --> 16:54.918
really happy then.

334
16:57.287 --> 16:58.522
Still…

335
17:00.357 --> 17:03.961
trusting others takes courage.

336
17:06.296 --> 17:07.197
Does it?

337
17:08.499 --> 17:12.603
I tend to expect a lot from people.

338
17:13.604 --> 17:14.438
Because

339
17:14.972 --> 17:17.007
everyone has something I don't.

340
17:18.942 --> 17:19.910
Miyamoto,

341
17:20.677 --> 17:22.646
maybe don't toss it all,

342
17:24.214 --> 17:25.549
but try tossing a little?

343
17:25.616 --> 17:26.984
Toss a little?

344
17:31.255 --> 17:35.392
What? Matsuzawa hasn't taken any of his comp days…

345
17:36.093 --> 17:36.927
‎HEAD COACH'S OFFICE

346
17:36.994 --> 17:37.895
What is it?

347
17:37.961 --> 17:40.130
There's something I'd like to talk to you about.

348
17:48.005 --> 17:49.173
Some of the runners

349
17:49.806 --> 17:52.709
don't seem very into this camp.

350
17:53.477 --> 17:54.611
In today's practice,

351
17:54.678 --> 17:57.381
they didn't seem to be running for real.

352
17:59.750 --> 18:01.118
One of them has

353
18:01.185 --> 18:04.555
a 10,000-meter personal best in the 28-minute range.

354
18:04.621 --> 18:07.791
They should've been competing near the front today.

355
18:09.359 --> 18:10.928
Should I talk to them?

356
18:12.996 --> 18:14.431
Running is interesting.

357
18:16.300 --> 18:19.303
What's buried deep in your heart shows itself.

358
18:21.305 --> 18:23.507
The team has only just started.

359
18:27.144 --> 18:28.378
There's still time.

360
18:37.354 --> 18:38.455
That's the length.

361
18:39.823 --> 18:41.024
Toyama.

362
18:41.925 --> 18:43.093
Oh, are we done?

363
18:43.160 --> 18:44.695
Then I'll go buy lunch!

364
18:44.761 --> 18:46.230
That's not it.

365
18:46.296 --> 18:48.198
-What do you think? -Think?

366
18:49.132 --> 18:50.000
The video segment.

367
18:51.201 --> 18:52.035
What?

368
18:53.070 --> 18:54.104
No…

369
18:56.173 --> 18:57.040
It's perfect.

370
18:58.542 --> 18:59.910
You can say what you really think.

371
19:00.911 --> 19:01.778
What?

372
19:05.882 --> 19:06.917
Well…

373
19:09.052 --> 19:11.955
if I had to say,

374
19:12.856 --> 19:16.026
the lead-up to the qualifying race results

375
19:17.394 --> 19:20.264
feels a bit short.

376
19:20.330 --> 19:23.267
I kind of want to stay on the edge of my seat longer.

377
19:24.968 --> 19:26.436
Give it a try.

378
19:27.337 --> 19:28.305
Give what a try?

379
19:28.372 --> 19:31.508
Cut it so it's more suspenseful.

380
19:31.575 --> 19:33.343
Me?

381
19:33.410 --> 19:36.446
The results will be announced shortly.

382
19:40.617 --> 19:41.451
Um…

383
19:42.185 --> 19:44.788
-Can you hold this shot a little longer? -Okay.

384
19:48.225 --> 19:50.494
I extended it by two seconds.

385
19:52.663 --> 19:55.365
Um, I think it's done.

386
19:56.900 --> 19:59.703
It's not suspenseful. It just drags.

387
20:00.837 --> 20:02.973
There's too much buildup before the announcement,

388
20:03.040 --> 20:05.175
so the key part afterward falls flat.

389
20:05.242 --> 20:07.644
And then what we want viewers to see…

390
20:09.112 --> 20:10.480
Sorry…

391
20:14.885 --> 20:16.153
Let's have lunch.

392
20:19.289 --> 20:20.123
Sorry,

393
20:20.190 --> 20:22.059
the rice balls were sold out…

394
20:23.760 --> 20:27.097
NATTO ROLL

395
20:27.164 --> 20:28.765
Natto Roll.

396
20:30.067 --> 20:33.036
-Sorry? -My nickname in elementary school.

397
20:34.404 --> 20:35.472
Natto Roll?

398
20:36.039 --> 20:36.907
Yeah.

399
20:38.542 --> 20:41.345
Um, why Natto Roll?

400
20:43.180 --> 20:45.582
-At first, it was Iron Mask. -Iron Mask?

401
20:46.283 --> 20:47.517
Because I wasn't friendly.

402
20:49.119 --> 20:51.488
Somehow that became Tekka Roll,

403
20:52.389 --> 20:55.092
and then it evolved into Natto Roll.

404
20:55.158 --> 20:56.493
Evolved…

405
20:57.294 --> 20:58.662
You know how that goes, right?

406
20:58.729 --> 21:01.198
Do I…?

407
21:04.468 --> 21:05.402
So,

408
21:06.002 --> 21:08.638
you've been an Iron Mask since you were a kid.

409
21:10.941 --> 21:12.109
Oh, sorry.

410
21:15.612 --> 21:18.315
Old habits die hard, I guess…

411
21:19.649 --> 21:21.852
Um, did something happen?

412
21:21.918 --> 21:23.854
You're acting weird today.

413
21:25.789 --> 21:27.657
-Let's eat. -Okay.

414
21:33.964 --> 21:35.732
Eyes straight ahead!

415
21:35.799 --> 21:37.367
Don't let the pace drop!

416
21:37.434 --> 21:38.735
Um, that's…

417
21:38.802 --> 21:40.637
Yeah. Coach Nagura.

418
21:40.704 --> 21:45.208
He's in his 20th year as head coach and led Kanto University to five titles.

419
21:45.275 --> 21:46.476
Mr. Maeda!

420
21:47.043 --> 21:48.445
Long time no see!

421
21:48.512 --> 21:50.514
Hey, it's been a while. How are you doing?

422
21:50.580 --> 21:52.315
-Not bad. -Oh.

423
21:52.983 --> 21:54.885
More importantly, did you get a little older?

424
21:54.951 --> 21:56.253
Hey,

425
21:56.319 --> 21:58.655
that's harsh right off the bat, Ono!

426
21:58.722 --> 22:00.157
Wow, they really love him.

427
22:00.924 --> 22:02.759
Mr. Maeda's research is on another level.

428
22:03.326 --> 22:05.796
From the finer points of the relay to each runner's personality,

429
22:06.396 --> 22:08.365
no one matches him in volume or depth.

430
22:08.865 --> 22:11.735
That's why he's been the broadcast announcer for six years.

431
22:11.802 --> 22:13.236
I see.

432
22:14.838 --> 22:15.772
Toyama.

433
22:15.839 --> 22:16.673
Yes?

434
22:16.740 --> 22:18.842
Do you know how many announcers work on Hakone?

435
22:20.177 --> 22:21.278
Um…

436
22:23.180 --> 22:24.247
Twenty-four.

437
22:25.449 --> 22:28.251
And every one of them trusts him completely.

438
22:28.318 --> 22:30.620
Right… Coach…

439
22:30.687 --> 22:32.489
With him as broadcast announcer,

440
22:33.156 --> 22:36.193
we can broadcast Hakone with confidence.

441
22:36.259 --> 22:37.694
Wow.

442
22:38.462 --> 22:41.865
Hakone's amazing. Twenty-four announcers alone.

443
22:42.999 --> 22:44.601
How many staff are there in total?

444
22:44.668 --> 22:48.872
When I was about your age, I was in charge of ordering staff lunches.

445
22:48.939 --> 22:51.575
Even back then it was 900 meals, so there must be more people now.

446
22:51.641 --> 22:53.176
Nine hundred?

447
22:53.243 --> 22:55.579
Hakone is an insanely huge broadcast.

448
22:55.645 --> 22:56.746
But, yeah.

449
22:56.813 --> 22:59.549
It's reassuring to think there are 900 pros working on it.

450
23:05.856 --> 23:06.857
Last 2,000!

451
23:07.424 --> 23:09.526
-Build it up! -Focus!

452
23:09.593 --> 23:12.329
-Focus! -One lap, 3:20.

453
23:18.635 --> 23:19.803
Let's pick it up from here!

454
23:20.370 --> 23:21.438
Almost there!

455
23:27.744 --> 23:30.213
-A press release? -Yes.

456
23:30.280 --> 23:33.116
I want to tell the media that the Student United Team

457
23:33.183 --> 23:34.317
has started training.

458
23:35.552 --> 23:37.921
Wouldn't it be better not to do anything too flashy?

459
23:39.823 --> 23:41.992
I want to draw even a little attention

460
23:42.058 --> 23:43.560
and boost the runners' morale.

461
23:44.361 --> 23:47.230
I want to do everything I can for that.

462
23:49.065 --> 23:50.667
If you really want to boost morale,

463
23:50.734 --> 23:54.404
wouldn't it be smarter to set a more realistic goal?

464
23:56.406 --> 23:57.908
Top three.

465
23:58.842 --> 23:59.910
With a goal like that,

466
23:59.976 --> 24:03.246
it's no wonder some runners feel they can't keep up.

467
24:05.482 --> 24:07.484
It's completely unrealistic.

468
24:07.984 --> 24:09.352
In the first place,

469
24:09.886 --> 24:12.489
winning isn't even the point of the Student United Team.

470
24:14.090 --> 24:16.459
I can't support training that puts

471
24:16.526 --> 24:18.094
needless strain on the runners.

472
24:19.963 --> 24:22.198
Well, there are all sorts of opinions,

473
24:23.199 --> 24:25.335
but Kai is the head coach of the Student United Team.

474
24:26.002 --> 24:27.137
The press release

475
24:27.203 --> 24:28.138
sounds fine.

476
24:29.005 --> 24:30.006
Let's give it a shot.

477
24:31.675 --> 24:32.842
Thank you.

478
24:38.348 --> 24:39.549
-Mr. Nomura. -Huh?

479
24:39.616 --> 24:42.686
Did you send Ms. Miyamoto your draft questions for the runners?

480
24:42.752 --> 24:44.421
Oh, right.

481
24:46.523 --> 24:48.258
What a pain…

482
24:50.126 --> 24:52.662
Sorry, Miyamoto.

483
24:52.729 --> 24:55.398
Can the questions for Yamanashi Gakuin

484
24:55.465 --> 24:56.499
wait a bit?

485
24:58.668 --> 25:01.204
No, you don't need to send them.

486
25:01.271 --> 25:02.105
What?

487
25:02.872 --> 25:04.708
Aren't you checking them?

488
25:05.675 --> 25:06.743
I'm not.

489
25:07.644 --> 25:08.979
You're not?

490
25:10.146 --> 25:12.115
I'll leave it to you, Mr. Nomura.

491
25:12.182 --> 25:13.683
Leave it to me?

492
25:14.250 --> 25:15.118
Yes.

493
25:19.189 --> 25:20.323
Okay then.

494
25:22.292 --> 25:23.126
See you.

495
25:23.193 --> 25:25.528
-Good work. -Good work.

496
25:25.595 --> 25:27.364
-Good work. -Oh.

497
25:27.864 --> 25:30.433
Mr. Tokushige, did you see this?

498
25:30.500 --> 25:31.468
What is it?

499
25:31.534 --> 25:33.370
A press release from the Student United Team.

500
25:34.104 --> 25:35.171
Huh?

501
25:35.805 --> 25:36.940
It's rare

502
25:37.007 --> 25:39.409
for the Student United Team to put out something like this.

503
25:39.476 --> 25:41.511
Their enthusiasm is great and all,

504
25:41.578 --> 25:43.780
but is this even news?

505
25:43.847 --> 25:46.916
The Student United Team is just a thrown-together bunch of weeds.

506
25:46.983 --> 25:48.618
Weeds…

507
25:48.685 --> 25:50.420
Could you drop by their practice?

508
25:50.487 --> 25:52.622
What, go there?

509
25:52.689 --> 25:55.091
I'm swamped with other interviews.

510
25:55.158 --> 25:56.126
Can you go?

511
25:56.192 --> 25:58.361
-The one on the list-- -No.

512
25:58.428 --> 26:00.196
The Student United Team

513
26:00.263 --> 26:01.931
passes the sash too.

514
26:03.133 --> 26:04.401
Understood.

515
26:04.467 --> 26:05.802
Which director should go with him?

516
26:06.369 --> 26:07.704
Let's see…

517
26:08.605 --> 26:09.639
I'll go!

518
26:10.240 --> 26:11.641
Wait, will Toyama be okay?

519
26:13.376 --> 26:14.210
Please go.

520
26:15.178 --> 26:16.346
Okay!

521
26:20.717 --> 26:22.819
Run! Don't lose your rhythm!

522
26:22.886 --> 26:25.588
Next, how do you feel heading into the main race?

523
26:26.222 --> 26:29.959
We have great talent on this year's Student United Team.

524
26:30.026 --> 26:31.895
I think we'll get results we can be happy with.

525
26:32.529 --> 26:33.563
By the way, Coach Kai,

526
26:34.497 --> 26:37.467
no one's ever started a coaching career

527
26:37.534 --> 26:39.469
with the Student United Team.

528
26:39.536 --> 26:40.470
That's right.

529
26:41.004 --> 26:44.474
It's your first time leading a team as head coach.

530
26:45.108 --> 26:48.211
And you have to unite runners from different schools.

531
26:48.912 --> 26:50.213
That must be really tough.

532
26:50.780 --> 26:52.449
It's a challenge worth taking.

533
26:53.349 --> 26:54.284
I'll make this team

534
26:55.485 --> 26:56.786
unlike any before.

535
26:57.620 --> 26:58.655
I see.

536
26:59.556 --> 27:01.357
Like never before.

537
27:01.958 --> 27:03.526
Well, I'm looking forward to it.

538
27:04.894 --> 27:07.363
-Stop the camera. -Oh, right.

539
27:08.431 --> 27:11.434
Next, we'll interview the runners.

540
27:11.501 --> 27:12.635
-Thank you. -Let's go.

541
27:12.702 --> 27:13.570
Okay.

542
27:16.806 --> 27:20.210
That guy's looking down on us.

543
27:20.777 --> 27:22.545
Let people who want to think that

544
27:22.612 --> 27:23.913
go on thinking it.

545
27:24.948 --> 27:27.550
It'll make things more fun.

546
27:28.952 --> 27:30.153
You'll see.

547
27:31.221 --> 27:32.255
You jerk!

548
27:34.958 --> 27:36.126
We're rolling!

549
27:36.793 --> 27:38.261
Um…

550
27:39.195 --> 27:40.830
from Toho University of Economics…

551
27:42.365 --> 27:43.566
Daichi Murai.

552
27:45.902 --> 27:48.938
You're from Akita, right, Mr. Sawada?

553
27:49.005 --> 27:50.006
Aomori.

554
27:50.774 --> 27:52.475
Sorry, Aomori.

555
27:52.542 --> 27:54.010
Well, it's all Tohoku.

556
27:54.077 --> 27:56.546
That took longer than I thought.

557
27:57.614 --> 27:59.516
That's enough on the Student United Team.

558
27:59.582 --> 28:00.850
We got the runners' comments.

559
28:00.917 --> 28:02.552
Sorry!

560
28:02.619 --> 28:05.221
I think I left the lens cap! I'll go back!

561
28:05.288 --> 28:07.824
Oh, yeah? Then sorry, I'll head back first.

562
28:07.891 --> 28:08.725
What?

563
28:08.792 --> 28:09.659
Good work.

564
28:10.260 --> 28:11.094
To Dainichi TV.

565
28:14.631 --> 28:15.999
What?

566
28:19.936 --> 28:20.904
It's not here.

567
29:00.176 --> 29:01.711
I'm sorry! I filmed you without asking.

568
29:03.546 --> 29:05.849
Um, you're from Dainichi TV…

569
29:06.416 --> 29:07.483
I'm Toyama.

570
29:08.184 --> 29:10.186
I forgot something.

571
29:13.623 --> 29:14.991
Hold on a second.

572
29:18.394 --> 29:19.729
‎This one…

573
29:23.299 --> 29:25.335
-Is this it? -That's it!

574
29:25.902 --> 29:28.004
-Sorry. Thank you. -I'm glad.

575
29:28.504 --> 29:29.472
Well, see you.

576
29:32.675 --> 29:35.879
I was there at the qualifying race!

577
29:36.679 --> 29:38.615
Meisei came so close.

578
29:40.183 --> 29:42.852
It was just ten seconds, right?

579
29:52.061 --> 29:53.863
We couldn't close…

580
29:55.965 --> 29:57.433
that ten-second gap…

581
30:03.806 --> 30:04.707
Well then…

582
30:16.319 --> 30:18.054
TO RUN

583
30:27.797 --> 30:28.698
What?

584
30:29.866 --> 30:30.900
I…

585
30:32.702 --> 30:34.237
messed up.

586
30:34.804 --> 30:35.638
What did you do?

587
30:36.539 --> 30:39.876
I said something insensitive today

588
30:40.510 --> 30:42.211
to Aoba from Meisei Gakuin.

589
30:43.313 --> 30:44.414
What did you say?

590
30:46.049 --> 30:47.150
I said it was a shame

591
30:48.251 --> 30:51.688
they missed by just ten seconds.

592
30:53.756 --> 30:55.191
And he said,

593
30:56.159 --> 31:01.297
"We couldn't close that ten-second gap."

594
31:03.199 --> 31:06.736
For them, ten seconds is enormous.

595
31:07.603 --> 31:12.008
I can't forget the look on his face.

596
31:13.876 --> 31:16.446
It wasn't just frustration.

597
31:17.146 --> 31:20.817
He looked like he was really hurting.

598
31:24.988 --> 31:26.055
That's because

599
31:27.290 --> 31:29.058
he's not running alone, Toyama.

600
31:29.692 --> 31:30.660
What?

601
31:30.727 --> 31:33.062
A Hakone Ekiden team has ten runners, right?

602
31:34.530 --> 31:37.400
What runners care about most

603
31:38.368 --> 31:41.938
is passing the sash to the next runner.

604
31:44.640 --> 31:47.744
His poor run at the qualifying race

605
31:48.511 --> 31:50.413
kept his team out of Hakone.

606
31:51.848 --> 31:54.751
In other words, it's his fault

607
31:54.817 --> 31:57.720
his teammates couldn't pass the sash.

608
31:57.787 --> 32:01.324
That's what he probably thinks.

609
32:04.827 --> 32:07.997
That makes what I said even worse.

610
32:10.133 --> 32:12.235
But isn't that okay?

611
32:14.771 --> 32:17.774
You noticed it and reflected on it.

612
32:19.108 --> 32:21.611
Now just use that next time.

613
32:24.414 --> 32:28.084
That's the first time you've ever praised me.

614
32:28.151 --> 32:30.520
-I wasn't praising you. -Thank you!

615
32:30.586 --> 32:32.922
I'm so motivated right now!

616
32:34.157 --> 32:35.291
I'll knock out

617
32:35.358 --> 32:37.460
your insanely long list of brutal fixes

618
32:37.527 --> 32:38.861
in no time!

619
32:38.928 --> 32:40.329
Oh, okay.

620
32:41.130 --> 32:44.067
-Okay, then. I'll leave the rest to you. -Okay!

621
32:53.576 --> 32:54.777
Man,

622
32:54.844 --> 32:58.214
today's Dainichi TV interview got me so pumped!

623
32:58.281 --> 33:01.851
I told them to film me from the right,

624
33:01.918 --> 33:04.987
but it was kind of more from the front.

625
33:05.054 --> 33:06.789
Don't worry. Nobody's going to watch it.

626
33:06.856 --> 33:07.957
Hey.

627
33:08.458 --> 33:10.359
Captain, what did they ask you?

628
33:11.994 --> 33:13.396
They asked about our team goal

629
33:14.063 --> 33:15.298
and…

630
33:15.898 --> 33:18.000
how I feel going into the main race.

631
33:18.067 --> 33:21.938
Whoa. You told them the team goal?

632
33:24.941 --> 33:26.442
-Was that bad? -No.

633
33:27.710 --> 33:30.179
I'm just amazed you could say it.

634
33:31.547 --> 33:33.449
Top three and all.

635
33:34.917 --> 33:37.120
-What's that supposed to mean? -No.

636
33:37.720 --> 33:38.721
Nothing.

637
33:43.059 --> 33:45.194
I've been thinking this since yesterday.

638
33:46.162 --> 33:49.198
Haven't you four been rude all along?

639
33:51.667 --> 33:52.835
I thought so too.

640
33:53.503 --> 33:55.238
You're ignoring Coach Kai's instructions.

641
33:55.304 --> 33:56.472
Of course we are.

642
33:56.539 --> 34:00.176
You'd have to be crazy to take this training seriously!

643
34:00.243 --> 34:01.878
I agree.

644
34:01.944 --> 34:05.748
Nobody's heard of the Student United Team having camp every week.

645
34:07.717 --> 34:09.051
He's never even been a head coach.

646
34:09.118 --> 34:11.954
I think he's just dragging us into his crazy dream.

647
34:13.089 --> 34:13.956
First of all,

648
34:14.524 --> 34:16.592
what does that coach know about us?

649
34:21.330 --> 34:23.533
What exactly are you unhappy about?

650
34:25.201 --> 34:27.336
He hasn't even explained his coaching philosophy.

651
34:28.271 --> 34:31.707
I want to spend a year aiming for next year's Hakone.

652
34:33.442 --> 34:34.744
A top-three finish

653
34:35.511 --> 34:36.946
with this team is unrealistic.

654
34:37.647 --> 34:39.849
The team's times aren't official.

655
34:41.751 --> 34:43.052
No official standings either.

656
34:43.653 --> 34:44.487
I'm fine

657
34:44.987 --> 34:48.457
as long as I run a race I'm satisfied with.

658
34:49.325 --> 34:51.861
If I get injured from overtraining, what's the point?

659
34:51.928 --> 34:52.929
But…

660
34:53.896 --> 34:57.567
you're all here because you want to run Hakone, right?

661
34:58.267 --> 35:01.304
Once you've decided that, shouldn't you follow the coach?

662
35:01.370 --> 35:02.438
No.

663
35:04.941 --> 35:07.343
The whole point of the Student United Team

664
35:09.011 --> 35:12.548
is to let as many runners as possible

665
35:12.615 --> 35:14.917
experience Hakone, right?

666
35:16.485 --> 35:17.687
So isn't it enough

667
35:17.753 --> 35:21.991
if each of us runs a race we're happy with?

668
35:22.058 --> 35:23.459
This team stuff

669
35:24.660 --> 35:27.196
is beside the point!

670
35:29.098 --> 35:32.935
If we're going to run, shouldn't we take it seriously?

671
35:33.769 --> 35:34.870
Right.

672
35:34.937 --> 35:37.340
In the end, that's what's good for you.

673
35:38.007 --> 35:39.141
Spare me the pretty speeches.

674
35:45.181 --> 35:47.149
-Um… -Yes?

675
35:49.051 --> 35:50.987
If you don't like it, why not just quit?

676
35:52.755 --> 35:55.124
You're dragging us down when you just jog along.

677
35:57.893 --> 36:01.030
I don't want to hear that from a guy with a 29-minute best.

678
36:03.499 --> 36:04.433
-Say that again. -Jo!

679
36:04.500 --> 36:05.468
-Jo! -I'll say it again.

680
36:05.534 --> 36:07.270
Don't wanna hear that from a 29-minute guy.

681
36:07.336 --> 36:09.505
Think slacking off in practice is cool?

682
36:09.572 --> 36:11.107
Don't you have any pride as a runner?

683
36:11.173 --> 36:12.875
-Back at you. -Huh?

684
36:12.942 --> 36:14.877
You're getting cocky over a ridiculous goal.

685
36:15.845 --> 36:18.080
So who's the one with no pride?

686
36:18.147 --> 36:19.849
Don't mess with me!

687
36:19.915 --> 36:21.484
-I'm not! -Knock it off, Jo!

688
36:21.550 --> 36:23.219
-Both of you… -Stop it!

689
36:23.286 --> 36:25.121
-…calm down! -Break it up!

690
36:30.760 --> 36:32.261
At the recent qualifying race…

691
36:36.532 --> 36:37.600
It was ten seconds.

692
36:41.537 --> 36:43.139
If we'd cut just ten seconds,

693
36:45.374 --> 36:47.076
our school would've made Hakone.

694
36:54.450 --> 36:56.252
I don't want it to end like this!

695
36:58.454 --> 36:59.388
I can't let it!

696
37:01.757 --> 37:03.292
Isn't it the same for all of you?

697
37:05.895 --> 37:07.930
You couldn't run Hakone with your teammates.

698
37:09.065 --> 37:13.169
It hurt, and that's why you came here, right?

699
37:15.738 --> 37:17.740
Sure, the Student United Team

700
37:18.307 --> 37:19.909
gets no record and no position.

701
37:20.710 --> 37:22.745
Maybe our sash doesn't carry much weight.

702
37:24.280 --> 37:25.114
But…

703
37:28.150 --> 37:29.151
even so,

704
37:30.686 --> 37:32.521
I want to run Hakone with this team!

705
37:36.659 --> 37:38.861
We've lost once already!

706
37:40.496 --> 37:42.298
That's exactly why we can run like no one else!

707
37:44.400 --> 37:45.701
I think Coach Kai…

708
37:48.604 --> 37:51.173
truly believes in guys like us.

709
37:56.512 --> 37:58.047
I want to run Hakone!

710
38:01.016 --> 38:01.984
With this team

711
38:03.686 --> 38:05.054
and with Coach Kai.

712
38:08.958 --> 38:09.925
Captain.

713
38:11.794 --> 38:13.696
I get…

714
38:15.498 --> 38:16.532
how you feel.

715
38:19.902 --> 38:23.239
But we're going to do things

716
38:24.540 --> 38:25.708
our way.

717
39:13.522 --> 39:14.356
Haruka.

718
39:17.026 --> 39:19.995
I was looking for you. There's something I want to show you.

719
39:23.432 --> 39:24.266
Here.

720
39:24.333 --> 39:25.668
ACHILLES TENDON STRETCHES

721
39:25.734 --> 39:26.769
Stretches?

722
39:27.369 --> 39:29.905
You hurt your left Achilles tendon once as a second-year,

723
39:29.972 --> 39:33.242
and again before the qualifying race.

724
39:35.411 --> 39:39.548
Coach Kai said to get in good shape for Hakone without overdoing it.

725
39:42.017 --> 39:44.653
I found some good videos. I'll send them to you.

726
39:46.989 --> 39:48.958
Haruka! Stay! Don't speed up now!

727
39:49.625 --> 39:52.261
…35, 36, 37…

728
39:54.096 --> 39:54.964
How does

729
39:56.165 --> 39:57.399
Coach know

730
39:57.466 --> 39:58.901
about my old injuries?

731
40:00.603 --> 40:02.838
Before camp, Coach Kai apparently visited

732
40:02.905 --> 40:06.308
the head coach at every school.

733
40:07.109 --> 40:09.512
-Every school? All 15? -Yes.

734
40:10.045 --> 40:12.047
Not just your running style

735
40:12.114 --> 40:16.218
and your health. He looked into personal things too.

736
40:17.119 --> 40:20.022
Like your family background and what foods you like.

737
40:22.725 --> 40:24.360
He said he wanted to really get to know

738
40:24.860 --> 40:27.162
each and every runner.

739
40:44.079 --> 40:46.181
‎TOHO ECONOMICS

740
42:01.857 --> 42:02.725
Um…

741
42:08.297 --> 42:11.467
I don't want it to end like this.

742
42:16.839 --> 42:19.908
I want to run for real…

743
42:23.979 --> 42:25.414
with the Student United Team too.

744
42:36.058 --> 42:38.293
Captain, you're way too early!

745
42:40.963 --> 42:42.731
Huh? Haruka?

746
42:43.332 --> 42:45.267
-Morning. -Good morning.

747
42:46.535 --> 42:47.836
Morning.

748
42:47.903 --> 42:49.004
What are you two

749
42:49.071 --> 42:50.572
doing out here so early?

750
42:51.507 --> 42:52.341
Well…

751
42:52.941 --> 42:55.778
We just kind of decided to run together.

752
42:56.745 --> 42:59.682
Just kind of?

753
42:59.748 --> 43:02.251
Well…

754
43:02.751 --> 43:05.354
All right! Everyone, let's warm up!

755
43:05.421 --> 43:06.388
Warm up!

756
43:06.955 --> 43:09.892
Why are you suddenly shouting? We know.

757
43:09.958 --> 43:11.694
Let's do it.

758
43:12.294 --> 43:13.662
It felt like everything

759
43:13.729 --> 43:15.764
was on my side today.

760
43:15.831 --> 43:17.800
My running was perfect too.

761
43:19.835 --> 43:22.504
50 DAYS UNTIL HAKONE EKIDEN

762
43:22.571 --> 43:24.339
I just watched it.

763
43:24.406 --> 43:26.608
Toyama's video is a lot better, right?

764
43:26.675 --> 43:28.043
I'd give it 70 points.

765
43:28.777 --> 43:30.979
I'll make revision notes for the other 30.

766
43:32.481 --> 43:33.682
‎REVISION NOTES

767
43:33.749 --> 43:34.983
More notes again?

768
43:35.050 --> 43:36.251
Revision notes?

769
43:36.318 --> 43:38.687
Whether it's a website video or broadcast,

770
43:38.754 --> 43:40.789
it should be done the same way.

771
43:40.856 --> 43:42.191
I just sent it to Toyama.

772
43:42.691 --> 43:44.893
Trusting people is different

773
43:44.960 --> 43:46.729
from giving up on them.

774
43:47.362 --> 43:50.099
But don't scare her too much, okay?

775
43:52.768 --> 43:53.769
But I think Toyama

776
43:54.570 --> 43:56.472
is cut out for Hakone.

777
43:57.606 --> 43:58.440
What's this?

778
43:59.541 --> 44:01.009
STICK WITH IT!

779
44:02.211 --> 44:03.712
A natto roll.

780
44:04.279 --> 44:06.782
-What does that mean? -Nothing.

781
44:07.683 --> 44:09.451
What is that?

782
44:24.233 --> 44:25.834
Hello? What's up?

783
44:29.571 --> 44:30.439
What?

784
44:31.507 --> 44:32.741
Okay, got it.

785
44:33.475 --> 44:35.344
Call me as soon as you know the details.

786
44:37.279 --> 44:38.113
Yeah.

787
44:39.815 --> 44:40.883
Is something wrong?

788
44:43.218 --> 44:45.320
Maeda will probably be hospitalized.

789
44:45.387 --> 44:47.623
‎KANTO UNIVERSITY

790
44:51.960 --> 44:53.295
This is bad…

791
44:56.131 --> 44:57.132
50 DAYS TO HAKONE EKIDEN

792
44:57.199 --> 44:58.066
THIS DRAMA IS A WORK OF FICTION.

793
44:58.133 --> 44:59.635
Honestly,

794
44:59.701 --> 45:02.271
-this broadcast is in real trouble. -I said this isn't a joke.

795
45:02.337 --> 45:04.072
Can I have a word?

796
45:04.139 --> 45:05.007
Subtitle translation by: Ken Nishizaki
